WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.149
When English actor Henry Cavill was announced
as the man who would lead Netflix's The Witcher,

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:09.000
starring on the Lauren Schmidt Hissrich -created
series as the rugged monster-hunter Geralt

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.350
of Rivia, the internet had a collective breakdown.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.870
Fans of The Witcher — both the collection
of books written by Andrzej Sapkowski and

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:20.279
the CD Projekt Red-backed video game series
based upon those creative works - were equal

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:23.800
parts stoked and skeptical about Cavill portraying
such a beloved character.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.769
"You look like trouble."

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:26.909
"Dead wrong.

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:28.609
I make trouble go away.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:30.390
I'm a witcher."

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.040
How well would he embody the silver-haired
beast-slayer?

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.690
Was he the right fit to play the part?

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.910
Would he prove himself a worthy witcher?

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:40.980
Despite the show's grim and intriguing trailers,
some people may have needed a bit more time

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.320
to be convinced that Cavill was absolutely
the right choice for Geralt — but one person

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.700
who knew from the get-go that Cavill was the
one was Lauren Schmidt Hissrich herself.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.760
Looper recently spoke with Hissrich, who opened
up about how Cavill landed the role of Geralt

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.760
in The Witcher.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.180
According to Hissrich, one reason why Cavill
was a clear choice to portray Geralt of Rivia,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.829
who undergoes physical training and intense
mutations to become a witcher at Kaer Morhen,

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:07.570
was because he was already well-versed in
Witcher lore - book series and video game

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.030
franchise alike.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.030
Hissrich admitted:

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.630
"Henry is a huge fan of the game.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.190
We met very early in the process and he had
played all of the video games.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.640
He's quite the gamer himself.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.070
And when he heard that Netflix was making
a show based on the books, he went out and

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.610
read all of the books."

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.280
Though the Netflix series hadn't started the
casting process when Hissrich and Cavill met

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.850
one another, Hissrich "loved his passion and
enthusiasm" for The Witcher.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.860
When Hissrich hired directors and writers
and began penning scripts for the series,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.990
that's when casting officially began.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.850
Hissrich explained that her approach to casting
is always, quote, "to see everyone, to see

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.880
different levels of actors from all over the
world - people who are very well-known [and]

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.140
people who've never been on television before."

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.890
She and the Witcher team wanted to ensure
that they were, quote, "exploring every avenue

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.690
to find the best Geralt," and in the end,
Hissrich met with "207 potential Geralts."

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.619
So, how did Hissrich land on Cavill after
seeing hundreds of actors gunning for the

00:01:57.619 --> 00:01:58.750
role of Geralt?

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.010
As she tells it, she simply couldn't get Cavill's
voice out of her mind while other actors auditioned

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.010
to lead The Witcher.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.010
Thus, Hissrich knew Cavill was the perfect
man for the job.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.310
"People call you a monster too."

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.840
"Why not kill them?"

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.290
"Because then I am what they say I am."

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.290
Hissrich shared:

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.420
"At the end of the day, though, I realized
that I actually kind of had Henry Cavill's

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.760
voice in my head, and I called him and explained
basically this whole thing and he was like,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.700
'Great, if you want me, I want you to know
that I'm the right person.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.010
I want you to have seen everyone and met everyone.'

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.500
And we met up again and we talked for several
hours.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.550
And by the time we left that day, I knew that
I had found our Geralt."

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.200
As mentioned, Cavill had something of an advantage
going into The Witcher through his extensive

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.900
knowledge of the source material.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.069
As most already know, Netflix's The Witcher
is based on Andrzej Sapkowski's books rather

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:48.170
than on the video game series' story, so Cavill
knew he had to brush up on the narrative behind

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.880
the narrative of the games he played through.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.000
Now, if the thought of having to read a ton
of text before the December 20 premiere of

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.900
The Witcher in order to enjoy and understand
the series has suddenly sent a shiver down

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.490
your spine, take a deep breath - Hissrich
has assured that the show will satisfy existing

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.750
Witcher fans without pushing away people who
are unfamiliar with the property.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.440
No matter if you've played the Witcher games
or have never picked one up before, read everything

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.520
Sapkowski has written or haven't gotten around
to doing so, it seems you'll be pleased with

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.710
Netflix's The Witcher.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.880
“Do not tell me that this is finally the
moment that you've decided to actually care

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.270
about someone other than yourself."

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:21.319
"Don't touch Roach."

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:22.319
She dished:

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:25.290
"The show has to appeal to and surprise existing
fans.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.129
That's our core enthusiastic bait.

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:30.840
Part of what I've loved in working on the
show is getting on, at first, Twitter and

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.480
then Instagram and even finally I got on Reddit
recently [and] really sort of delving into

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.430
the fandom and seeing what they like.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.000
What I tried to do is take the stories that
they loved and they were familiar with, but

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.340
to tell them in a different way, which is
part of the structural changes that you'll

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.790
actually see in the show.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.470
We're sort of [saying], 'Let's basically present
this story in slightly different dressing.'"

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.390
She continued, noting that the series' creative
team kept in mind not to, quote, "alienate

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.280
people who've never heard of The Witcher before,"
and made careful decisions to ensure that

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:03.569
those new to the property can enjoy the upcoming
series just as much as longtime fans.

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:04.569
Hissrich explained:

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:07.790
"You want to make sure that there's things
for them to fall in love with, too.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.820
For instance, in the first episode, we only
meet Geralt and Ciri I found that in trying

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.970
to explain what a witcher was and to learn
all of these new names and all of these new

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.140
places and things that sound unfamiliar and
trying to wrap your head around the politics

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.990
of the world, what I want to do is hold off
introducing Yennefer because I wanted to make

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.300
sure I was giving it in bite-sized pieces.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.110
And then what that allowed us to do, in episode
two, [was] to let both Ciri and Geralt take

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.460
a slight backseat and really delve into Yennefer
and to make sure we understand where she's

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.610
coming from."

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.280
In Hissrich's words, The Witcher is, quote,
"really a mix of both - trying to make sure

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.110
that the story is engaging for fans, but also
really exciting for people who've never heard

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.110
of the project."

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:48.180
No doubt that Henry Cavill's handsome mug
will also pull viewers into the show, the

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.400
first eight episodes of which hit Netflix
on December 20th.

